A||||9||hugh||88Gt v8 rear springs||||1||
Z||000000||hugh||06-21-2000||09:33 PM||||I just got a 1988 GT with a 345HP small block crate engine about 2 weeks ago.It has a Toronado transaxle.Looking at the underneath of the back of the car I only have about 3 inches clearance between the pan and the ground.I just saw the rear springs have spacers in them.I want to get the specs for the springs so I can work out what springs from other cars might be a suitable replacement.I've called spring suppliers all over the country and it is as if the 88 was never built.Nobody has the specs.,not even Pontiac. HELP!!!!  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||06-21-2000||10:20 PM||||For about $200 you can go with adjustable coil-overs and put in some 350 -400 Lb springs and the make your ride height anything you like. Rayne motorsports has good  info on spring rates.||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||06-21-2000||10:25 PM||||This is a strut suspension,How can you use coilovers?Where is Rayne Motorsports located?Thanks,HUGH||||reg||1
Z||000003||olympic||06-22-2000||04:51 AM||||  Using a coilover suspension involves replacing the struts with struts that have a narrower body and 2.5" ID "coil-over" springs  A side benefit of this conversion is that you free up some wheel well room for wider tires.<P>   Their site is <A HREF="http://www.ryanemotorsports.com" TARGET=_blank>www.ryanemotorsports.com</A>  and I think they are in Oregon.<P>   BTW, do you have any 0-60 or 1/4 mile times for your Fiero.  It sounds bad ass.||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||06-22-2000||10:50 AM||||You might try some springs from a large FWD Buick,or other GM product of the same body style.I'd tried a set way back;fit great,just way too stiff for my car. <BR>Might be a quick fix,until you can get a coilover kit from Ground Control(new springs of your choice,and a threaded sleeve/collar,for easy height adjustments).<BR>Does the car do wheelies?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||06-22-2000||06:15 PM||||Using Raynes kit  you don't have to buy new struts just "CAREFULLY" cut the spring perch off with a dremel and slide on the threaded collar||||reg||1
Z||000006||hugh||06-22-2000||09:09 PM||||I called Raynes today,They say they they can  supply whatever I need.(Bielstein struts with coilovers will cost $768.00)Thats a lot of money,but if you want the best and consider how long they last then the cost per mile,or day,or trip isn't so bad.Thanks for the help.HUGH||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||06-22-2000||09:39 PM||||A cheaper alternative, and the way I went with my 1985 V8 Fiero, was to buy the RCC rear coil-over conversion kit ($395 I think). I believe the had the 88 kits for the same price. The RCC kits are a bolt in with Gabriel gas struts pre-installed. Trouble is, their website went down months ago, I don't know if that means bad news or what. With a engine conversion, get the rear coil-overs. Those Ryanne kits are top notch, but a little pricey. Sometimes you get what you pay for though.||||reg||1
Z||000008||VISCERAL||06-23-2000||12:10 AM||||try <A HREF="http://www.groundcontrol.com" TARGET=_blank>www.groundcontrol.com</A> <BR>350# springs <BR>3" lift or drop||||reg||1
Z||000009||Standard||06-23-2000||05:15 AM||||Well.. technically.. the rear struts <B>are</B> coil-overs... just not adjustable.  Just my part to confuse everyone.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
